00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:34.680
GANG CHEN: OK, so first
I think you should start

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.520
thinking about the project.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:45.600
And what I'm looking
for is a short report,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.490
paper-type report.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:51.420
I want a limit of
only five pages.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:58.890
And it could be anything
related to this subject.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.420
So if you happen to be
working on something,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.390
you want to extend and
apply the knowledge

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.880
that you learned
in this subject,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.900
you're welcome to do so.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:16.020
And in fact, I
wrote a paper based

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.740
on term paper I was working on
when I was a graduate student.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.850
I thought that that
was a good experience.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:26.220
In fact, it turns out
to be the first paper

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.410
on thermal conductivity
of quantum wells.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:34.300
I was taking a
linear optics course.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.150
I spent the whole semester.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.750
And at the end, I
went to professor,

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.860
I said, I don't
understand what you talk.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.360
And he wanted a term paper.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.205
And I said, how about--

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.130
he was talking about
[INAUDIBLE] the Feynman diagram.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.280
So I still don't quite
understand what [? is ?]

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.170
the Feynman diagram.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.317
But so I said, how
about I worked on paper

00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:01.650
on quantum wells, thermal
conductivity of quantum wells.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.233
He said, fine.

00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:03.220
That's fine.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.590
So I wrote the term paper.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.400
And I continued to work
on it, and I eventually

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.700
actually published a paper
on the thermal conductivity

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.470
of quantum wells.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.550
And looking back at there,
because of the-- let's say

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.680
I was doing pretty much
a Boltzmann equation.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.030
So completely forgot
about quantization.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.170
But it turns out the most
of the stuff were correct.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.120
Not everything was correct.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.290
And so what I want
to say is I encourage

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:41.340
you to think about subject
related to your research.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.650
It can expand that.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.420
Or if, say, you
don't have a subject,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.970
you can do a literature review.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.220
And then say you're interested
in a specific topic,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:59.670
let's say metamaterials,
or plasmonics, or any

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.900
of the things that we
mentioned or you read,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.180
you are going to
apply the knowledge.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.590
You can do a literature
review and expand on it.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.600
Maybe you say,
OK, based on that,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.420
maybe this is an
interesting problem

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.190
that I can continue working on.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:22.470
You can work after
this semester.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:26.100
You're always welcome
to discuss with me.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.490
And if you really don't-- let's
say you don't have a topic,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.080
come talk to me.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.470
I think I can always
figure something.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.820
We can negotiate and see
whether it would be interesting.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.810
OK?

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.890
So that's the term paper.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:56.680
So now you have all turned in
your first midterm and redo it.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:05.080
Any questions on
those first midterm?

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.900
I think I say, when I
was looking at where

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.990
were most of the mistakes made,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:22.240
I think one of this is the
depths of the evanescent wave.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.360
And probably because unless you
go back-- you've written down,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.990
and it may not be easy to
figure out what's the depth.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:36.100
But the key is you should
be able to write down

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.220
the plane wave expressions.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.270
And if you have the
plane wave expression,

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.930
and you use your Snell's law,
and you can find the angle

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.360
when the angle cosine theta--

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.970
or sine theta is larger than 1.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.260
So you use your cosine theta
1 squared root 1 minus sine

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.110
theta squared, and they
plug in the plane wave,

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:03.010
you should be able to find
out what's the penetration

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.570
depth of the evanescent wave.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:11.290
And there was another
question about what's

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.970
the number of molecules
have a certain speed.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.520
That's a gas molecule.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.250
And so there, I
would guess probably,

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.490
you maybe get [INAUDIBLE]
what's the total number of--

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.190
you need to start with the
total number of molecules

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.100
per unit volume.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.500
And that's the first value you
need to-- given temperature,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.740
given pressure, you could
use the ideal gas law

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.110
to find out what's
the local density,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.640
and then use the Maxwell
velocity distribution to see

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.370
what's the total number, or
what's the number of molecules

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.970
that have a certain velocity.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.590
And then I think it's
a matter of time.

00:05:55.590 --> 00:06:00.090
Most of you couldn't finish
the last problem, where

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.780
we're looking at the phonon--

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.440
the equivalent of
phonon distribution

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.070
to the black-body
radiation, where the peak

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.100
Wien's displacement is null.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.620
So you find out that in many
cases, a low temperature, yeah,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.040
you do have a peak.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.315
But at high temperature, that
wavelength, peak wavelength,

00:06:24.315 --> 00:06:27.990
is too short compared to
times the lattice constant.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.020
So you actually never
reach that peak.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:37.960
It will be-- you should
continue to try that out.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.850
And for many years,
I haven't done

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.710
that myself until this
time, because I have

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.180
to make a problem out of it.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.210
This is the optical absorption.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.120
That's the second
longer problem,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.950
what's the optical absorption
for a direct gap semiconductor.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:01.510
And it's a matter of
joint density of states.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:05.340
So if I have--

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.890
so I have a
semiconductor dispersion,

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:09.490
and I have a photon.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:14.130
This is the [INAUDIBLE]
electron from the valence band

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.870
to conduction band.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.040
And then you need to say, this
is my photon energy, h omega--

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:20.820
mu.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.130
And how is it related to k?

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.500
So you need to
first write down--

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.500
because you know this
parabola, this parabola,

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.480
and the difference
of these two energy

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.670
gives you the photon energy.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:38.100
So once you have photon
energy or frequency versus k,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:44.160
and you can use the normal
way we do density of states,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.790
because the absorption depends
on how many states you have.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.280
And this is, in fact,
called the joint density

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.030
of states between the valence
band and conduction band.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:57.570
And then it turns out it's the
same as your bulk material.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:02.770
That's the square root
of E minus the band gap.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:11.590
So the absorption of the light
for a particular semiconductor,

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.290
this is--

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.500
this curve of absorption
is a reflection

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.840
of the joint density of states.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.810
And of course, you
have the population.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:26.970
We didn't say, OK, you have to
say how many electrons here,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.800
how many empty states here.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.280
If I do it, I really
just say this is the F,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.340
and here F for the number
of electrons here is empty.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.620
So that's a 1 minus F.
But that's a different F,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.220
because there-- if your
chemical potential is here,

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:50.790
and you say the Fermi-Dirac is,
say, energy minus mu, and here

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:54.510
is the valence band
energy minus mu there.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:09:00.780
So basically, if
you force yourself

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.720
going through those
problems many times,

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.090
you can understand a lot
of the basics that you see.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.370
And I did not do this before.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.830
And this time I said, OK,
I'll give you problems.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:20.850
I have first to do it myself.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:21.740
Any questions?

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.830
OK.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:28.480
So for now, let's come back.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:34.360
And if we think what we
were talking the last time,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.170
we talked about this
wave-to-particle transition.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:41.680
And from that, I
said, from now on, I'm

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.780
going to neglect the phase
of the energy carriers,

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.700
the plane wave and--

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.200
I'm no longer caring
the phase, I'm

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.230
just looking at the
trajectory and treat them

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:58.390
as [INAUDIBLE] so looking
at the trajectory.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:01.660
And for that, we say--

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:05.000
we discussed briefly
the Louville equation,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.680
which is the N particle
distribution function.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:12.670
And that is, of course,
the N is a huge number

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:14.980
of particles in my system.

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:18.280
And we say, OK, from
this Louville equation,

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.200
there are generally
two approaches.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:26.470
One is the Boltzmann equation
that [INAUDIBLE] developed.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:30.680
And the other is the
linear response theory,

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.370
which I hope I will have time
by the end to cover some.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:35.680
If you do molecular
dynamics, you

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.170
should really dive into
linear response theory.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.250
That's the analysis.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:43.570
I commented a lot of
people, in fact, say,

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.430
any undergraduate
student coming in

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:50.350
can conceptually understand
F equal ma, do a simulation.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:53.560
But the key is you have
to really understand

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.740
the basis for that.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:05.920
And so we move on and say,
OK, from f to N And to f of 1,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.320
that's one particle
distribution function.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.570
That's the Boltzmann equation.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:14.860
And the f N is just
the multiplication

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.010
of f1 for N particles.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:25.870
And because of the one
particle, now this particle

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:29.380
will interact with the rest of
the particles in this system.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:34.150
So the Boltzmann equation,
which you roughly

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.630
can show from Louville
equation, the left-hand side

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.610
is df dt the first derivative,
because here is still

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:45.040
a function in phase space.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:48.740
f is the distribution function,
still function in phase space.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:54.860
So the first derivative is
gradient with respect to space.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.910
The second gradient is with
respect to the momentum.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:08.310
And it's actually interesting
to comment, the f,

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:12.600
it looks like a function
of x, y, z, vx, vy, vz.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.360
It looks like it has a
direction. vx, vy, vz,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.760
if a particle is going
in a certain direction,

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.910
I have a different f.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:29.440
But the question is,
it's a vector or scalar?

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:31.740
What do you think?

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:36.070
It's a scalar in high
dimensional space.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.070
And if you just think about
the-- any point in space

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:42.590
and particle going
in specific direction

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:44.220
have specific distribution.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.580
So that looks like it has
directional properties.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.530
But that directional
property is really

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.825
the [? wave ?] coordinate, the
higher dimensional coordinate

00:12:51.825 --> 00:12:52.940
in space--

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.440
phase space coordinate.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:55.940
OK.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.320
And also, there are different
ways to write this fourth term.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:03.560
dp dt is the same--

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.110
if you look at, think about
the [? Louville's ?] law,

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.320
dp dt gives you
the F, the force.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:15.110
And the force, you can
write F, because p is ma--

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:21.410
mv, so I can put an m out and
f, say, derivative with respect

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:26.300
to v. Well, p is
a function of hk.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:30.260
So I can use
different variables.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:33.380
Rather than using
p, I can use v,

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:37.820
I can use K, and a different
way of writing these terms.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:40.700
And that's most
of the time, say,

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:42.680
when you deal with
the electrons,

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.150
you have to keep this term.

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:49.470
That's the important driving
force for the electron motion.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.640
When we deal with
photon or phonon,

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.930
we do not need this term or
even, say, the molecules.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.390
There's no direct force on them.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:06.530
But the computation is really
on this left-hand side--

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:09.970
right-hand side, where
I say now the particle

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.190
interacts with the
rest of the particle.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:14.780
And that's the collision term.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:20.350
So let's move on and try to
write down this collision term,

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:24.340
because so far it looks
all pretty simple.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:28.510
On the left-hand
side, you can say it's

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:32.150
the first-order derivative.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:42.290
And so let's look at the
collision term, df dt.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:43.790
This is not a derivative.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:48.080
It's just a symbol
how we can express it.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:52.310
And normally, I can
start just to consider

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:54.350
the two-particle collision.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:59.270
And so if I have two particles
interact with each other--

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.550
and this initially,
this particle

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:06.140
has an energy and
momentum or wave vector--

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:07.880
hk is the momentum--

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:14.960
and this other
particle has an E1 k1.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:19.640
And when they get close to
the interaction range, what is

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:21.090
the close to interaction range?

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:23.960
And so if you think
about two gas,

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:27.140
say, atoms, it's
the electrons when

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:31.310
the electrostatic potential
can interact with each other.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:35.780
So you have electrons,
you have nuclei.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:40.230
So you have-- that's where
they start to interact.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:44.530
So if you think about the
gas molecules in this room,

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:49.210
we see the average mean
free path of 100 nanometers.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:53.770
And the atomic size
is a few angstroms.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:57.820
So there is actually a very
large space in between them.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:59.860
Most times, they
do not interact.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.103
They will just travel freely.

00:16:02.103 --> 00:16:04.270
And when they're getting
really close to each other,

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:07.290
you get this collision.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:13.930
But due to the collision, you
have the energy change, maybe.

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:16.700
And you are changing
the direction.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:20.740
So that's the final state
for this first particle.

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:25.210
And you have the energy change
of second particle and momentum

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.490
change of the second particle.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:35.130
And in a typical
problem, what you want

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.210
to say, OK, what's the first--

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.890
what's the rate of--
what's the probability?

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.020
What's the rate?

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:49.580
And the way to do this
is a proximate method.

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:50.980
So if you have--

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:53.840
originally, if you
solve a method--

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:57.430
for example, if I have a photon
comes in and getting absorbed

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:02.170
by the materials-- getting
absorbed by the material,

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:06.339
so originally, the material
has a certain energy,

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:07.970
and that's, quantum
mechanically,

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:10.480
I have these solutions,
allowable energy levels.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.800
That's what we did in
the second chapter.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:19.480
So original problem, this
system, before interacting,

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:20.589
has an energy.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:26.109
And correspondingly, we can
write the Schrodinger equation.

00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:28.720
Essentially, we can
write a Hamiltonian,

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.920
which is the kinetic energy.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:36.130
So h bar squared 2m k--

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:40.450
so it's the operator, that's the
first term in the Schrodinger

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:42.800
equation--

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:45.530
and plus u0.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.170
So when we solve the
Schrodinger equation,

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:58.290
we say this one equals mass
i h bar d [? plus i ?] dt.

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:02.030
We solved for the energy states.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:05.240
So now what I
have-- so this is--

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.420
we did the steady state problem.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:12.650
Now, during photon
comes in, what it does

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:15.050
is to create a perturbation.

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:17.990
You treat it as a
small perturbation

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:25.400
to the original Hamiltonian, H.
So let's say this is your H0.

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:29.300
And the small
perturbation, let's say now

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:33.890
the interaction
Hamiltonian H is H0

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:42.060
plus a small perturbation, which
could be a function r and t.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:42.560
All right.

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:46.410
This is the interaction term.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.540
This is the original
unperturbed,

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:51.000
where I already have--

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:55.650
supposedly, you already have the
Schrodinger equation solution.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:57.180
You know the energy level.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:59.430
You know the wave function.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:02.970
It's the quantum state
of the original system.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:04.860
And due to this
perturbation, you

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.660
go back to the
Schrodinger equation,

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:10.340
you solve it using
perturbation method.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.110
We're not going to do it.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:15.730
And with that, you
can find the rate

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:18.880
of perturbing due to
this perturbation,

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:23.510
the system changing from
one state to the other.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:27.040
And this rate is
the transition rate.

00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:41.800
This is from the initial
state to a final state.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:46.630
And this is a 2 pi h bar.

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:53.980
And we have the integration of
the final state wave function

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:57.100
complex conjugate
the perturbation.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:20:02.430
So this is the
unperturbed system,

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:04.590
the wave function--
let's suppose

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.560
you start with a certain
initial state wave

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.660
function and the final
state wave function.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:14.670
Let's suppose you already have
solved the original problem.

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:17.640
And now, given
this perturbation,

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:22.050
Hamiltonian, and I have the
initial state wave function,

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:23.850
and I interact--

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:30.200
integrate over the volume
of that interaction.

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:33.880
And this is squared,
and then a delta

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:43.620
function Ei minus Ef, which
says the initial energy,

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:46.740
the energy conservation
delta function

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:52.200
is 0, when the Ei
does not equal Ef.

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:55.890
So only when Ei equals
Ef, this is non-zero.

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.430
So essentially, what this
gives us is the energy

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:03.890
conservation of the system.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:06.400
And if you do this,
if you see, when

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:10.630
you do the wave
function for plane wave,

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:12.550
you have wave vector.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:15.380
For each quantum state,
you have wave vector.

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.350
And if you do your
integration of this,

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:22.540
you'll also find a corresponding
concentration in wave vector.

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:25.400
That's your momentum
conservation.

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:29.110
So what you have,
essentially you

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:31.780
will get the momentum
energy conservation

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:34.240
from this transition rate.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:42.880
And so they-- you have, for
example, the initial energy.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:46.610
So this is the
two-particle interaction.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.570
So this is initially,
E is a function of k.

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:58.240
And the incoming one, E1
k1, equals E prime k prime.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:02.830
It goes scatter
into another state,

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:09.250
and the E1 prime k1 prime.

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:11.170
So that's the
energy conservation.

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:13.630
This is that delta function--

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:19.000
the initial of the two systems,
two particles, and the final

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:21.940
of the two particles.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:25.720
And the corresponding
energy conservation

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:32.500
is k plus k1 prime
equals k prime--

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:37.390
that's the final--
plus k1 prime final.

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:41.470
And let's say, just
with-- [? hold ?] on this.

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:47.020
And because hk, hk, that gives
me momentum, later on I'll

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:50.800
make of a modification
to this form.

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:54.770
Here can say this is a
simple momentum conservation.

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.550
But later on, we'll
add-- so in crystal, you

00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:59.740
add reciprocal lattice g.

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:03.220
And that's a so-called
Umklapp process.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:09.190
But basically, those transition
rates also give you--

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:11.620
in doing the math,
also you actually

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:16.080
get the energy and the
momentum conservation.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:20.820
And most of the time,
you can say people do not

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:23.100
like to write those integrals.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.620
That's why the Dirac, if you
read the quantum mechanics

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.680
book, they will
write Dirac notation.

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:34.230
And the Dirac notation for
this is your initial state

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:35.400
interacting--

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.800
that's like a wave
function here--

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.800
interaction with
perturbed Hamiltonian

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.550
and goes to a final state.

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:44.640
So that represents
this integral.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:49.280
And you have this integration.

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:50.330
OK.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:56.555
And you also write this as
a matrix form, f i squared.

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:01.780
So there are three different
ways to do quantum mechanics.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:06.610
One is the matrix, do the
Heisenberg; Dirac notation,

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:13.320
Dirac; and the Schrodinger
is the Schrodinger equation.

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:17.000
They are all equivalent, OK?

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:21.390
So this is the famous
Fermi golden rule.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.660
Next time you heard Fermi
golden rule, you'll know this.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:25.740
And this we have said.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:41.150
So that gives you the
transition rate from the initial

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:43.590
to final with--

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:48.470
given the interaction
Hamiltonian.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:53.180
This is by showing the time
dependent Schrodinger equation

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:54.530
using the perturbation method.

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:58.470
OK.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.630
So that's the first step.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:05.070
We say we're dealing
with the collision.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:09.090
And now coming back to
the Boltzmann equation,

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.880
I'm looking at the one particle.

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:16.470
And this particle can
collide with many particles

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.450
in the system.

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:25.820
So I have to count all
the other possibilities.

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:32.650
And so the-- now I can start
writing df dt, this collision

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:39.780
term, df dt, this collision.

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:45.690
First, if this particle is
collide to other states,

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:48.270
then I have a loss, right?

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:54.930
It goes from original E k
state to E prime k prime state.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:26:00.150
Then this f, because f is
represented in the one particle

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:05.770
at a specific state, so I
lose a particle in that state.

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:08.160
So my first term is going out.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:09.030
That's a loss.

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:11.130
So I have a negative sign.

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:12.480
So it's going out.

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:22.940
And of course, that depends on,
say-- the rate really depends--

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:28.220
say first, I have the rate here,
collision rate for two quantum

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:31.550
states, but then I need to say
in each quantum states, how

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:35.090
many particles I have.

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:46.340
So I need to say f, really
the initial t, r, k,

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:54.130
and if it collides with
the other one, t, r--

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:55.730
at the same location--

00:26:55.730 --> 00:27:01.160
k1, so how many in
k, how many in k1?

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:08.420
And what's the rate
of going from k, k1,

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:14.100
to k prime k1 prime?

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:17.660
So this is where the Fermi
golden rule tells me those are

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:22.880
how many to start with, OK?

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.520
So this is a loss.

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:29.000
And now because I'm
counting one specific,

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:35.000
so I need to really, say, sum
up all other possibilities.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:39.680
So I need to sum up k1,
I need to sum up k prime,

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:42.820
I need to sum up k1 prime.

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:46.060
That summation is actually--
because each k is a wave

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:51.460
vector, is a vector,
actually each of these k's

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:53.430
is three summations.

00:27:53.430 --> 00:28:03.620
So I have really nine
summations. k1 is a vector,

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:09.200
k prime is a vector, and
k1 prime is a vector.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.590
This is a loss.

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.190
And then there is the gain.

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:21.520
That's going from the reverse.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:26.110
Say, how many k prime, k1
prime scatter and scatter back

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:31.770
into the current state of k?

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:41.360
So the gain is from t, r--

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:45.290
so here, this is where
the Boltzmann equation,

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:49.540
where the molecular
chaos assumption we

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:50.590
used before, right?

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:53.810
So it's only interacting
at our point.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:56.230
So I'm not writing
a different r point.

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.090
I mentioned that there
are modification.

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:02.350
People try to consider final
size of the atom or molecule,

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:04.420
like [INAUDIBLE] equation.

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:08.230
In that case, you have to
either two different r points.

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:11.060
That becomes a lot
more complicated.

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:15.220
So here I have k prime.

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:19.570
That's from the initial
state is k prime.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:25.860
And t, r, same, and k1 prime.

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:32.940
And they will interact and going
from k prime, k1 prime going

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:37.440
to k, k1.

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:40.890
But what I care is really
just this one specific quantum

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:41.970
state in k.

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:49.030
So I need to sum, again,
all those other states.

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:54.670
So this is k1, k
prime, k1 prime.

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:56.940
So this is summation
index, as I said.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.370
So this is out, this is in.

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:03.200
I'm doing the number balance.

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:09.130
And you put in that summation
into the left-hand side,

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.050
right-hand side of the
Boltzmann equation,

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:16.240
you gather the full
Boltzmann equation, OK?

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:21.430
Now you can say this
was just a starter.

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:24.490
The real thing is
the computation

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:29.280
is on the right-hand side.

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:34.870
And most times, we do not
write this as a summation.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:37.380
You can use what
you learned, how

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:41.080
to change your summation
into integration.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.660
k is a sum in the
quantum states.

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:47.020
Now you need to convert that
summation into integration.

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:49.600
As long as all
the assumptions we

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:54.040
made apply here, so if
you convert this sum

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:56.140
issue into integration,
what do you have--

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:57.850
in terms of the number
of integrations?

00:31:00.574 --> 00:31:01.940
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:02.900
GANG CHEN: Line.

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:04.385
Each of this is a vector.

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:09.630
So you have [? line ?]
nested integrals.

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:19.180
And of course, when you
convert state, you do 2l--

00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:21.700
the distance is 2 pi over l.

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:23.830
So you have that distance.

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:27.640
You have all this constant
from converting summation

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:29.170
into integration.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:31.480
And you have [? line ?]
nested integrals.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:37.460
And this [? line ?] nested
integrals is d k1, a vector.

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:38.590
So I can write three.

00:31:38.590 --> 00:31:43.020
There's d k1 x, d k1 y, d k1 z.

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:48.225
And d k prime and d k1 prime.

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:57.420
So this is from converting
summation to integration.

00:31:57.420 --> 00:32:04.670
The second comment is
this W. It turns out

00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:08.450
that one could argue that
this W equals this W, that's

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:10.510
a detailed balance.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:13.990
And in equilibrium, everything,
[? say, ?] is the same--

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:16.480
scatter out equals scatter in.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.210
Otherwise, you
have this one state

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:21.920
either will be
depleted completely,

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:24.310
or it goes to below to infinite.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:26.440
That's the detailed balance.

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:29.020
So because of that,
you have the equality.

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:31.750
So you write it and write in two
terms you can split-- write it

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:34.070
into-- combine into one term.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:41.440
So at the end, what I can
write is scattering term df dt,

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:43.130
say, equals--

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:46.060
and I put a negative sign in
the first term in the front.

00:32:46.060 --> 00:33:02.800
I have then W and f t,
r, k and f t, r, k1--

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:05.470
so this is the
first term-- minus--

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:07.780
let me write this here--

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:10.982
the second term is, say, f--

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:29.130
minus f t, r, k prime
and f t, r, k1 prime.

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:33.800
Let me just make
another comment.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:39.420
And here, I'm writing
two fixed particles.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:41.130
When you do a real problem--

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:43.560
whether, for example when
your electron interacts

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:46.890
with a phonon, at the
end, the electron--

00:33:46.890 --> 00:33:50.520
the phonon may be
absorbed, so you

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.590
don't have a phonon coming out.

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:55.030
So for each of
the specific case,

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:58.400
you need to carefully
write down this f.

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:01.540
Here I'm just
writing a generic way

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:05.960
of if I have two particle comes
in, collides with each other,

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:08.000
I have two particles goes out.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:12.230
But say, when you actually
have photon getting absorbed,

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:14.179
photon disappear.

00:34:14.179 --> 00:34:18.889
So you need to do each of this
case just using the same idea,

00:34:18.889 --> 00:34:20.750
do your detailed expression.

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:24.210
And so you read the different
papers, different problem,

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:27.980
this will be
different expressions.

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:28.730
OK.

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:30.199
So now I'm going to solve this.

00:34:30.199 --> 00:34:30.770
Yes?

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:31.940
AUDIENCE: Never mind.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:33.170
I was just wondering,
what's the point

00:34:33.170 --> 00:34:35.030
of having an integral
representation of this

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:37.620
when we're probably going to
be doing this numerically?

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:41.630
And then because this also
has to have the assumption

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:43.670
that these functions
are slowly varying

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:45.913
for every infinitesimal step--

00:34:45.913 --> 00:34:46.580
GANG CHEN: Yeah.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:53.659
So integral-- that's true.

00:34:53.659 --> 00:34:55.460
You can go back to summation.

00:34:55.460 --> 00:35:01.705
But then remember, in the
past, before computers

00:35:01.705 --> 00:35:03.080
were very powerful,
people always

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:06.380
tried to say whether I
can solve it numerically--

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:09.550
analytically.

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:13.015
But so that's the--

00:35:13.015 --> 00:35:16.030
and in fact, I say most of
the time this function doesn't

00:35:16.030 --> 00:35:17.510
change that rapidly.

00:35:17.510 --> 00:35:22.110
So that's a valid approximation.

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:25.760
It's not as bad as,
say, the line integral.

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:29.330
The reason is that, you see,
when you have collision,

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:36.140
you have these delta functions
So it's those delta function

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:38.570
in energy conservation.

00:35:38.570 --> 00:35:41.570
And the delta in
momentum conservation--

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:44.500
this is also delta function--

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:47.830
was significantly constrained.

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:51.835
What are the possible
values you have to consider?

00:35:55.010 --> 00:36:00.860
So that's the-- that will allow
you to simplify and make this

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:07.280
still [? trackable. ?] But I
have to say that solving full

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:09.560
Boltzmann equation-- now
I'm not going to write--

00:36:09.560 --> 00:36:14.750
combine the left-hand side
to the right-hand side,

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:17.610
left-hand side to
right-hand side--

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:21.080
what you really have
is a differentiable

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:26.390
on the left, integral
equation on the right.

00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:32.810
So even though you are solving
one specific state with k,

00:36:32.810 --> 00:36:36.110
you have to consider all the
other k's in your system, all

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.630
the quantum states
in your system,

00:36:38.630 --> 00:36:41.900
to solve these
distribution functions.

00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:46.940
And in transport, of course, if
your temperature is changing,

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:52.870
say as a function r, so every
point you consider all the k's.

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:57.160
And you solve-- so the
way that the people,

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:01.420
some people try to solve
for each of these fields--

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:03.570
there are not many--

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:04.770
twice.

00:37:04.770 --> 00:37:07.710
It's pretty complicated problem.

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:12.330
And let's say you typically
do an iterative solution,

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:16.320
because this one is
for a specific k,

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:21.370
but this integration
involves all other states.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:25.030
Your solution has to meet
all those requirements.

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:28.000
OK.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:32.620
So it's the integral
differential equation.

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:37.090
And the integration
is in k space.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:41.410
The differentiation
in both, say,

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:45.305
real space r and the p, k space.

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.590
But this is very hard.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:58.780
And this equation, the
Boltzmann equation,

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:01.450
is used by electrical engineers.

00:38:01.450 --> 00:38:04.450
It's the foundation you can
derive all the electrical--

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:07.510
the equations the
electrical engineers use.

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:12.010
And it's used-- a lot
of the past literature

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:16.130
is on gas dynamics,
rarefied gas dynamics.

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.550
A lot of studies in
rarefied gas dynamics.

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:24.530
Do you know the reason
why there are so

00:38:24.530 --> 00:38:26.000
many studies in gas dynamics?

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:32.630
This is due to Apollo Project.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:33.130
Why?

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:37.180
Because when you try
to send the spaceship

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:41.110
through the atmosphere, when
you go outside the [? IRC, ?]

00:38:41.110 --> 00:38:45.040
go to outer atmosphere,
the pressure is very low.

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:48.210
The molecule mean free
path is very long.

00:38:48.210 --> 00:38:51.910
So it's in the rarefied
gas flow regime.

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:58.070
So a lot of study in rarefied
gas dynamics in the 1960s

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:00.560
region, time scale.

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:04.160
Now they're studying neutrons.

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:08.170
You go to look at the
neutron transport,

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:11.470
there are a lot of works just
solving neutron Boltzmann

00:39:11.470 --> 00:39:12.350
equation.

00:39:12.350 --> 00:39:14.860
So you've got the neutron
Boltzmann equation,

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:17.390
you've got the gas
Boltzmann equation,

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:20.350
you've got the electron
Boltzmann equation,

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:25.840
you can do electron and
the phonon and photon--

00:39:25.840 --> 00:39:28.180
I teach another course,
radiation transfer.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:32.860
At the end, we solve this
equation for the Photons

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:34.750
that's when you
completely ignore

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:40.130
the phase of the
particles, of those waves.

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:42.100
So this is a--

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:45.007
that's why really, there
are not many equations.

00:39:45.007 --> 00:39:47.340
If you start from here, you
can derive all the equations

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:49.220
that every field,
or most of fields,

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:52.960
use in the transport regime.

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:56.790
But it's a molecular
chaos assumption.

00:39:56.790 --> 00:40:02.700
So this is only valid when
the particles are diluted.

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:05.460
That's why so far I've been
doing electron, phonon,

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:09.000
photon molecules more
on a parallel path.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:14.780
I can't deal-- you can't use
Boltzmann equation for liquid.

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:17.500
That's not right.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:19.900
OK.

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:24.110
Now, moving on, this is
a hard equation to solve.

00:40:24.110 --> 00:40:27.850
So there is a creative
way to make it simple.

00:40:27.850 --> 00:40:33.730
And that creative way, now we
have Boltzmann equation right,

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:36.550
so we do relaxation
time approximation.

00:40:50.520 --> 00:40:54.390
I say I can't deal with
these nasty integrals.

00:40:54.390 --> 00:40:58.095
So what I'm going to write is--

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:08.290
you'll be happy.

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:11.920
Forget about the
line integral, OK?

00:41:11.920 --> 00:41:13.495
This is the relaxation time.

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:27.350
OK.

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:31.440
And now you will feel that
we can probably do something.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:35.070
And this relaxation
time approximation--

00:41:35.070 --> 00:41:37.590
again, there are different
field has a different name.

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:40.290
You put in this relaxation
time on the right-hand side

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:44.700
of Boltzmann equation, you go
to rarefied gas dynamic, or gas

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:49.360
dynamic, but it will not be
called Boltzmann equation.

00:41:49.360 --> 00:41:52.330
It will be called
the Krook equation.

00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:56.440
So in the gas dynamic, that
will be Krook equation.

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:02.310
And in other
fields, people don't

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:03.560
call it the Krook equation.

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:06.240
So I remember once I
was giving a seminar.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:10.700
And a question was, oh, you're
not solving Boltzmann equation,

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:12.315
you're solving Krook equation.

00:42:12.315 --> 00:42:16.170
At least I know who is Krook--

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:17.680
what is the Krook equation.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:19.470
OK.

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:24.760
So the-- [? why ?] it's
the relaxation time.

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:29.290
And if you put in
the right-hand side--

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:32.040
and let's forget the
spatial dependence--

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:38.100
then what I have
is df dt equals--

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:42.450
so if your system is uniformly
disturbed from equilibrium--

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:47.430
f0 is the equilibrium
distribution--

00:42:47.430 --> 00:42:49.890
then you can stay
here, if you solve

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:54.960
this system will return
f to equilibrium,

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.370
f0 equals exponential
t over tau.

00:42:59.370 --> 00:43:03.510
So you can see this
exponentially relaxed back

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:06.050
to equilibrium.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:10.010
That's why tau is called
the relaxation time.

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:19.700
And typically, we call
this Boltzmann equation

00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:24.050
under relaxation
time approximation.

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:29.300
Sometimes, people also call the
linearized Boltzmann equation,

00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:33.300
but there are some
confusion there, I think.

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:40.200
So now let's go to discuss
what's the relaxation time?

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:42.650
What's the scattering?

00:43:42.650 --> 00:43:50.960
And let's look at the
different relaxation times,

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:52.957
different process, scattering.

00:43:55.580 --> 00:44:02.850
In general, when I think
about the scattering

00:44:02.850 --> 00:44:06.850
and the relaxation
process, of course

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:10.086
we can say-- we mentioned
before photon absorption.

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:22.020
So if I have--

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:25.920
before, we say, OK, this is
my-- the problem we worked on.

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:32.330
This is my semiconductor
with a bandgap, wave vector.

00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:34.520
So you know it's 3D.

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:41.540
And if I have a photon comes
in, excite from here to here,

00:44:41.540 --> 00:44:46.700
and the photon
momentum is very small,

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:48.760
so when I do my
energy conservation,

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:52.060
this is the electron
initial energy,

00:44:52.060 --> 00:44:54.070
and this is the final energy.

00:44:54.070 --> 00:44:58.330
And initial energy, you also
have the photon energy, right?

00:44:58.330 --> 00:44:59.320
It's a two particle.

00:44:59.320 --> 00:45:04.710
So initially, I have electron
at the E [INAUDIBLE] energy.

00:45:04.710 --> 00:45:09.640
And initially, my
photon has energy h nu.

00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:13.486
And then finally, the
electron has energy Ef.

00:45:13.486 --> 00:45:18.520
So I have my energy
conservation is Ef minus Ei.

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:21.610
This is the initial
electron energy that

00:45:21.610 --> 00:45:24.730
equals h bar omega or h nu.

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:29.840
That's the photon energy.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:35.840
And the momentum
is kf equals ki,

00:45:35.840 --> 00:45:39.470
so the final momentum
of the electron

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:44.585
plus the photon, h
bar k, of the photon.

00:45:49.940 --> 00:45:51.680
And of course, the
photon wavelength

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:53.810
is a micron, typically.

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:58.950
If 1 micron is 1.1 above
the 1 electronvolt,

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:04.360
the band gap is typically
electronvolt, few electronvolts

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:07.120
or 0.1 electronvolt.

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:10.900
So you can say that
the wavelength, lambda,

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:13.750
is of the order of 1 micron.

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:16.710
And here, the zone
boundary is pi

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:20.430
over A. A is the
lattice constant.

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:22.350
The lattice constant
is angstrom.

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:25.890
So you can say that, say, lambda
really doesn't shift this.

00:46:25.890 --> 00:46:29.220
The photon momentum that
does not shift the k much.

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:32.400
It's just very little change.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:36.450
So it's essentially a vertical
transition in this case.

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.715
And that's the homework
problem you did--

00:46:38.715 --> 00:46:43.510
no, the midterm
problem you did, right?

00:46:43.510 --> 00:46:45.370
And of course, you
can extend this.

00:46:45.370 --> 00:46:49.840
If you do an indirect gap
semiconductor like silicon,

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:54.000
and say, silicon I
have a valence band

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:57.450
here, conduction band
is not the minimum

00:46:57.450 --> 00:47:04.310
and somewhere here,
this is a silicon

00:47:04.310 --> 00:47:08.080
indirect gap in general.

00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:14.010
And now when the energy of the
photon is right at the gap,

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:16.005
you can say there is
not enough momentum.

00:47:18.760 --> 00:47:21.800
The photon cannot leave the
electron from here to here

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:27.440
along, because the
momentum k is not enough.

00:47:27.440 --> 00:47:31.360
So what it does is
it will interact--

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:34.740
you will actually
have three particles--

00:47:34.740 --> 00:47:37.590
an electron interacting
with the photon

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:43.080
and interacting with the phonon;
phonon, very small energy,

00:47:43.080 --> 00:47:46.540
milli-eV to tens of milli-eV.

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:50.030
And the wave vector,
on the other hand,

00:47:50.030 --> 00:47:52.330
the momentum is large.

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:54.290
So you can write your--

00:47:54.290 --> 00:48:00.420
in that case, you'll say,
OK, the indirect gap,

00:48:00.420 --> 00:48:04.870
if this transition happened,
it will involve both electron,

00:48:04.870 --> 00:48:07.530
phonon, and photon.

00:48:07.530 --> 00:48:14.640
Ef equals Ei plus
h omega, photon.

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:20.280
And it could be plus or
minus h bar omega, phonon.

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:24.750
So what the plus minus
means, if it's a plus,

00:48:24.750 --> 00:48:26.220
it absorbs a phonon.

00:48:26.220 --> 00:48:27.870
Phonon gets in.

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:29.895
If it's a minus,
a phonon emitted.

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:34.510
So that's your
energy conservation.

00:48:34.510 --> 00:48:37.000
Now you have three
particles involved.

00:48:37.000 --> 00:48:52.120
And kf equals ki plus h bar k
photon, which you can neglect.

00:48:52.120 --> 00:48:55.750
And in the case of
phonon, this energy is--

00:48:55.750 --> 00:48:58.700
still, you should carry
it in your analysis,

00:48:58.700 --> 00:49:00.590
but this one is pretty small--

00:49:00.590 --> 00:49:04.000
and plus minus h bar k phonon.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:15.430
So now if you can
do this problem,

00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:17.638
you should be able to
do this problem using

00:49:17.638 --> 00:49:18.430
the joint density--

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:20.950
try to write down this
joint density state--

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:25.300
joint density of states and
say, if you have an indirect gap

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:28.150
semiconductor, how
the absorption would

00:49:28.150 --> 00:49:30.160
change near the band gap.

00:49:33.310 --> 00:49:37.590
So those are the things
now you can apply.

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:41.690
And of course, what I say--

00:49:41.690 --> 00:49:45.230
what the general behavior
is for direct gap,

00:49:45.230 --> 00:49:48.620
this is your energy gap, Eg.

00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:55.180
And you have a very sharp
rise in the absorption.

00:49:55.180 --> 00:50:01.430
But the indirect gap, it could
be lower phonon absorption

00:50:01.430 --> 00:50:02.150
or higher.

00:50:02.150 --> 00:50:04.920
But the rate will
be much smaller.

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:11.550
And that's the-- we
mentioned before, this

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:16.500
is a problem of silicon when you
try to use it as a solar cell,

00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:20.770
because this absorption
is not very strong, which

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:24.720
[? typically ?] means you can
have to use very thick material

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:28.560
to absorb the photons
to capture it.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:33.100
And that is the cost
of the materials.

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:33.610
Yes?

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:36.760
AUDIENCE: Can you actually have
that free body interaction?

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:37.630
GANG CHEN: Oh, yeah.

00:50:37.630 --> 00:50:40.330
AUDIENCE: Or is it [INAUDIBLE]
first and the photon

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:43.620
has to provide the critical
gap, and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:43.620 --> 00:50:44.500
that energy?

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:47.030
GANG CHEN: Oh, that's a--
this is a very good question.

00:50:47.030 --> 00:50:48.550
It's a rate problem.

00:50:48.550 --> 00:50:49.270
AUDIENCE: Sorry?

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:50.650
GANG CHEN: It's a rate, right?

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:55.630
So whether it happened
if you have absorbed--

00:50:55.630 --> 00:51:04.540
and I think because you probably
can have some mismatch due

00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:07.060
to the uncertainty principle.

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:09.700
But I do not say--

00:51:09.700 --> 00:51:15.090
so you-- sometimes, you can say
you raise that virtual state.

00:51:15.090 --> 00:51:17.550
And then you need the
other thing to come in.

00:51:17.550 --> 00:51:19.500
But see, that's very short.

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:23.050
So almost instantaneously,
you have to have that.

00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:27.930
So that's another reason
why this one is smaller.

00:51:27.930 --> 00:51:31.455
The scattering matrix
will be weaker.

00:51:31.455 --> 00:51:35.980
And that's in this
region is smaller.

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:36.640
Yes?

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:39.440
AUDIENCE: If you were here to
provide a lattice vibration

00:51:39.440 --> 00:51:42.770
for indirect band
gap semiconductor,

00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:47.030
could you theoretically convert
it from indirect to direct?

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:48.380
GANG CHEN: How-- oh.

00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:50.218
Could you-- what's
the question again?

00:51:50.218 --> 00:51:52.010
AUDIENCE: Basically,
providing the phonon--

00:51:52.010 --> 00:51:53.960
GANG CHEN: Right, right.

00:51:53.960 --> 00:51:56.720
Well, you don't have--

00:51:56.720 --> 00:52:00.320
see, this is-- you
can't shift this one.

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:03.220
There are some ideas
how to shift it.

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:06.840
The idea to shift it is
using quantum size effect.

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:12.720
That's the first problem I give
in the quiz, the short problem.

00:52:12.720 --> 00:52:14.920
Now, when you use a very small--

00:52:14.920 --> 00:52:19.530
so what happens is
this one will rise,

00:52:19.530 --> 00:52:21.990
the next-- the first level
will become here rather

00:52:21.990 --> 00:52:23.410
than at the bottom.

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:25.530
And if it's small
enough, this one

00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:28.506
rises here and be
lower than that.

00:52:28.506 --> 00:52:31.095
That depends on
the effective mass.

00:52:31.095 --> 00:52:34.650
If this is a sharp, this
is a very, very-- see,

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:37.650
the flat means a
large effective mass.

00:52:37.650 --> 00:52:41.320
That means you need a much
smaller diameter to raise it.

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:46.090
So you have to look at the
effective mass of each band.

00:52:46.090 --> 00:52:48.340
And of course, when
you make small,

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:50.200
this will also going down.

00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:52.840
And some of you see this is--

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:54.640
the first problem
is that you have

00:52:54.640 --> 00:52:57.170
to consider what's going on
for the conduction band, what

00:52:57.170 --> 00:52:58.000
goes down.

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:00.400
When the multiply-- when the
effective mass is the same,

00:53:00.400 --> 00:53:02.170
you multiply it
by a factor of 2.

00:53:02.170 --> 00:53:06.010
That's how you
enlarge the band gap.

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:07.710
OK?

00:53:07.710 --> 00:53:12.050
So this is a photon--

00:53:12.050 --> 00:53:13.910
well, maybe I can
make one more comment

00:53:13.910 --> 00:53:16.710
and relate to
[INAUDIBLE] question.

00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:20.910
Some people will say, OK, let
me develop an intermediate band

00:53:20.910 --> 00:53:23.580
gap solar cell.

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:26.480
What does that mean?

00:53:26.480 --> 00:53:29.580
This is a band gap.

00:53:29.580 --> 00:53:32.280
And some of the
infrared photons is not

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:35.490
absorbed, because there's
not enough energy.

00:53:35.490 --> 00:53:39.450
So the idea is, OK, let
me develop a energy level

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:42.600
in the gap by putting in
other atoms or quantum

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:43.680
dots in the region.

00:53:46.280 --> 00:53:49.490
And then the infrared
photon now can go from here

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:52.520
to here, the first photon.

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:56.510
And then the second photon
goes from here to here.

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:58.460
It's a nice idea.

00:53:58.460 --> 00:53:59.870
That's hard to do.

00:53:59.870 --> 00:54:01.890
Why?

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:05.490
This is a matter of
time, residual time.

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.600
You need the electron
to here, and it

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:10.970
has a tendency to get lost.

00:54:10.970 --> 00:54:13.940
The competition,
this is all rigged.

00:54:13.940 --> 00:54:17.420
If it relaxes back or it
interacts with a phonon

00:54:17.420 --> 00:54:20.430
and becomes heat, then you lost.

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:24.830
So you have-- if you can
stay there long enough,

00:54:24.830 --> 00:54:28.970
wait for the next photon
to come to lift it,

00:54:28.970 --> 00:54:33.930
you can go to calculate
roughly, back of envelope,

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:36.620
if you have certain
photon density coming in,

00:54:36.620 --> 00:54:38.360
how long it takes
for the next photon

00:54:38.360 --> 00:54:40.130
to come in with
that certain energy.

00:54:43.550 --> 00:54:48.410
And the other way is,
say, there are also

00:54:48.410 --> 00:54:51.380
the so-called
two-photon absorption.

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:56.070
What happens is two photons
come in at the same time,

00:54:56.070 --> 00:54:59.940
at same place, and you
get that transition.

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:01.710
But you can see
in that case, what

00:55:01.710 --> 00:55:06.030
they need is a very high flux.

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:09.300
Because they need to come at
the same time and same location,

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:11.070
so you need a lot of it.

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:14.460
So nonlinear optics is
two-photon absorption.

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:16.620
They require very
high intensity.

00:55:16.620 --> 00:55:19.030
You use lasers to do that.

00:55:19.030 --> 00:55:24.150
And in fact, many times you need
a very high intensity lasers

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:27.100
to get nonlinear regime.

00:55:27.100 --> 00:55:29.770
So it's all related to the
rate, the time, when you

00:55:29.770 --> 00:55:32.690
think about what's possible.

00:55:32.690 --> 00:55:34.360
There's a concept there.

00:55:34.360 --> 00:55:38.710
This is rate concept
is very important.

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:42.520
How fast is the process?

00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:49.060
And you can dream, for example,
I have some electron comes in--

00:55:49.060 --> 00:55:52.450
photon comes in.

00:55:52.450 --> 00:55:55.090
I can lift the electron--

00:55:55.090 --> 00:56:00.620
sometimes I can have absorption
from here to here, free carrier

00:56:00.620 --> 00:56:01.970
absorption.

00:56:01.970 --> 00:56:06.290
And say, OK-- or I have a
high energy photons comes

00:56:06.290 --> 00:56:08.960
in, because the solar spectrum
have a lot of photons like

00:56:08.960 --> 00:56:10.510
that--

00:56:10.510 --> 00:56:13.090
you raise the electron here.

00:56:13.090 --> 00:56:16.930
Isn't it good if you take this
electronic energy out rather

00:56:16.930 --> 00:56:18.760
than go back to here?

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:22.640
Most solar cells, this
electron will go here,

00:56:22.640 --> 00:56:25.270
then you take it out.

00:56:25.270 --> 00:56:31.370
From here to here is about
a picosecond time scale.

00:56:31.370 --> 00:56:33.260
So you look at the
how long it takes--

00:56:33.260 --> 00:56:39.190
how 1 picosecond, how much
electron will travel, you say,

00:56:39.190 --> 00:56:42.050
that's very hard.

00:56:42.050 --> 00:56:47.360
So this rate process, those
are the very important,

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:48.740
getting this order right.

00:56:52.850 --> 00:56:57.680
So this is-- we say, OK, energy
transport, energy conversion.

00:56:57.680 --> 00:56:58.250
What is it?

00:56:58.250 --> 00:57:01.620
This is an energy
conversion, wouldn't you say?

00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:06.640
the energy is going
from photon to electron.

00:57:06.640 --> 00:57:09.180
And there are, of course,
many competing processes

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:11.160
if you do a device.

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:15.180
And the other processes are
electron-phonon once you

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:19.200
get the electron, and
the phonon-phonon.

00:57:22.530 --> 00:57:27.253
So let's look at phonon-phonon.

00:57:34.660 --> 00:57:39.340
So far, when we talk about
phonon, we did dispersion,

00:57:39.340 --> 00:57:43.840
we did a spring, mass-spring.

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:47.490
That's the idea of
harmonic oscillator.

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:51.180
And they do not interact if I
just have harmonic oscillator.

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:53.340
Photon is a harmonic oscillator.

00:57:53.340 --> 00:57:57.870
Photons do not interact, OK?

00:57:57.870 --> 00:58:03.120
So it's the unharmonic term
that will cause the interaction.

00:58:03.120 --> 00:58:06.930
And for phonon, in fact,
it's hard to get a photon

00:58:06.930 --> 00:58:09.360
talk to each other,
interact with each other.

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:13.560
Like I said, nonlinear optics,
you need a very large flux

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:15.600
to get them together, right?

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:19.350
But with a phonon, which is--

00:58:19.350 --> 00:58:20.970
in [INAUDIBLE], the lattice--

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:22.950
this is the potential
we draw before.

00:58:22.950 --> 00:58:27.220
And harmonic approximation is
approximately at this point

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:29.520
in my parabola.

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:33.300
So here is the potential,
here is the distance.

00:58:33.300 --> 00:58:37.500
And if I say, if you write
down the higher-order term--

00:58:37.500 --> 00:58:41.490
this is the minimum, and the
first order is [? gone ?]

00:58:41.490 --> 00:58:45.480
1/2 k x minus x0 squared.

00:58:45.480 --> 00:58:46.980
So that's the harmonic term.

00:58:49.750 --> 00:58:53.320
And then you have unharmonic--
the next term is unharmonic.

00:58:53.320 --> 00:58:56.740
So you expand your potential
using Taylor expansion.

00:58:56.740 --> 00:59:06.845
It's d d x square, say,
x0, and x minus x0 cubic.

00:59:06.845 --> 00:59:08.470
There is order, first
order [? gone, ?]

00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:11.890
second order, third order.

00:59:11.890 --> 00:59:14.940
You can write fourth order.

00:59:14.940 --> 00:59:17.330
Now you do your spring constant.

00:59:17.330 --> 00:59:22.920
Your force is df du minus dx.

00:59:22.920 --> 00:59:26.640
Of course you get your spring
term, harmonic spring term.

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:31.530
That's what we use when
do phonon dispersion.

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:35.060
But if you go to your next
term, your higher-order term,

00:59:35.060 --> 00:59:39.260
you'll get the x
minus x0 squared.

00:59:39.260 --> 00:59:40.860
That's the third-order term.

00:59:40.860 --> 00:59:45.200
So that gives you not a spring,
linear spring, a nonlinear

00:59:45.200 --> 00:59:45.700
spring.

00:59:48.880 --> 00:59:53.810
Now, if you have two
phonons, each one

00:59:53.810 --> 01:00:00.340
has one specific frequency,
you do their product

01:00:00.340 --> 01:00:02.080
of the two phonons.

01:00:02.080 --> 01:00:07.210
Omega 1-- sine omega 1 times--
omega 1 t times sine omega 2 t,

01:00:07.210 --> 01:00:08.450
what do you get?

01:00:08.450 --> 01:00:09.730
You get plus minus.

01:00:12.640 --> 01:00:13.910
That's a nonlinear term.

01:00:13.910 --> 01:00:16.240
So you start to have
the two sine waves

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:21.600
interact with each other if
you have those nonlinear terms.

01:00:21.600 --> 01:00:23.570
Otherwise, it's just a
linear superposition.

01:00:23.570 --> 01:00:26.730
They will never
change the frequency.

01:00:26.730 --> 01:00:32.480
So this is the source of
phonon-phonon interaction.

01:00:32.480 --> 01:00:36.950
And the unharmonic fourth
term gives you the part--

01:00:36.950 --> 01:00:41.670
because of this, you can see
if I have omega 1 comes in

01:00:41.670 --> 01:00:46.570
and omega 2 comes in,
you can go omega 3.

01:00:46.570 --> 01:00:47.780
That's a sum.

01:00:47.780 --> 01:00:53.240
So what it means is
two phonons become one.

01:00:53.240 --> 01:00:56.180
Or you can have one
phonon split into two.

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:05.270
So you have the omega
1, omega 2, omega 3.

01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:08.060
And then in the process,
you have energy and momentum

01:01:08.060 --> 01:01:09.230
conservation.

01:01:09.230 --> 01:01:16.070
This is h omega 1 plus h
omega 2 equals h omega 3.

01:01:16.070 --> 01:01:22.550
That's for-- this one is
the absorption process.

01:01:22.550 --> 01:01:25.250
And in phonon language, we
don't call that absorption.

01:01:25.250 --> 01:01:28.970
We say that annihilation.

01:01:28.970 --> 01:01:30.320
OK.

01:01:30.320 --> 01:01:34.400
See, maybe I should
not pronounce that H?

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:35.270
Annihilation.

01:01:41.780 --> 01:01:44.530
Annihilation, you agree?

01:01:44.530 --> 01:01:46.810
And here is a creation.

01:01:46.810 --> 01:01:49.250
One becomes two.

01:01:49.250 --> 01:01:51.155
So this is a creation process.

01:01:57.590 --> 01:02:02.970
So say, for this, you can
write a corresponding energy

01:02:02.970 --> 01:02:05.700
conservation.

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:08.940
Now, momentum conservation
is interesting.

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:15.090
And normally, I would
write h bar k1 plus h bar

01:02:15.090 --> 01:02:20.670
k2 equals h bar k3.

01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:22.820
This is normal.

01:02:22.820 --> 01:02:25.160
And in fact, this is
called normal process.

01:02:35.050 --> 01:02:37.320
OK.

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:40.770
But normal process,
that scattering actually

01:02:40.770 --> 01:02:43.320
does not create a
resistance for heat flow.

01:02:46.410 --> 01:02:47.060
Why is that?

01:02:51.390 --> 01:02:55.470
Let's think about the
vector format we just did.

01:03:01.890 --> 01:03:07.020
So what I have, if I draw that,
that's a momentum conservation.

01:03:07.020 --> 01:03:14.070
So this is a h bar k1, h bar k2.

01:03:17.190 --> 01:03:20.250
And this is when they
combined, they form h bar k3.

01:03:23.110 --> 01:03:25.280
That's normal process.

01:03:25.280 --> 01:03:29.090
Now, originally I have
h bar k1 is carrying out

01:03:29.090 --> 01:03:32.170
heat h bar omega 1.

01:03:32.170 --> 01:03:35.920
And h bar k2 is carrying
heat h bar omega 2.

01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:38.920
Now, if it's a normal
process, this final one

01:03:38.920 --> 01:03:42.520
is carrying energy of
omega 1 plus omega 2.

01:03:42.520 --> 01:03:45.790
That's the same as the original.

01:03:45.790 --> 01:03:49.330
And also, the direction
is the sum of the two.

01:03:51.860 --> 01:03:53.710
So essentially, the
[? lattice ?] effect

01:03:53.710 --> 01:03:56.860
is if I have heat flow,
this creates a negative flow

01:03:56.860 --> 01:03:58.060
in this direction.

01:03:58.060 --> 01:04:00.640
This is final one
is still carrying

01:04:00.640 --> 01:04:06.130
that same amount of heat
flow in the same direction.

01:04:06.130 --> 01:04:12.520
So this normal process actually
does not create a resistance.

01:04:15.560 --> 01:04:23.220
And what happens is if I
think about the dispersion,

01:04:23.220 --> 01:04:29.200
so phonon dispersion
transfers, not Schrodinger.

01:04:29.200 --> 01:04:33.810
And so you have
to consider phonon

01:04:33.810 --> 01:04:36.420
from each different branch,
and whether they can satisfy

01:04:36.420 --> 01:04:38.880
the energy conservation.

01:04:38.880 --> 01:04:46.160
This is k and the
specific direction omega.

01:04:46.160 --> 01:04:50.590
And sometimes, when this
one goes beyond the h k1,

01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:57.380
let's say, and here is k2,
k1, k2 go beyond the Brillouin

01:04:57.380 --> 01:05:00.160
zone, get a much larger k.

01:05:03.050 --> 01:05:07.550
And in that case,
the crystal symmetry,

01:05:07.550 --> 01:05:09.995
the reciprocal lattice
here, this point G--

01:05:12.830 --> 01:05:15.140
it could be another
direction G. I'm just

01:05:15.140 --> 01:05:16.640
drawing on the
same branch, but it

01:05:16.640 --> 01:05:21.680
could be a completely different
branch, two phonons interact.

01:05:21.680 --> 01:05:26.630
And if you do the wave
analysis, so what's better

01:05:26.630 --> 01:05:35.660
allowable process is h bar
k1 plus h bar k2 equals h bar

01:05:35.660 --> 01:05:44.300
k3 plus h bar G. This G is
the reciprocal lattice vector.

01:05:47.950 --> 01:05:53.950
in chapter 4, we talked
about the Fourier transform

01:05:53.950 --> 01:05:58.570
of crystal lattice point
into Fourier space.

01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:01.420
This Fourier space has
a corresponding lattice

01:06:01.420 --> 01:06:08.020
constant G. And
that G is that G.

01:06:08.020 --> 01:06:14.020
And this process, by
adding another vector G,

01:06:14.020 --> 01:06:17.840
you could go completely
different random directions

01:06:17.840 --> 01:06:20.720
that's not the original
high flow direction.

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:25.670
So with this process, it still
has the energy conservation,

01:06:25.670 --> 01:06:32.110
but the direction change due
to the crystal momentum here,

01:06:32.110 --> 01:06:33.990
this creates the resistance.

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:39.760
And this is called
the Umklapp process.

01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:48.940
It's a German word.

01:06:48.940 --> 01:06:54.350
I think-- I'm not sure, but this
explanation comes from Peierls.

01:06:54.350 --> 01:06:58.490
Peierls was a--

01:06:58.490 --> 01:07:01.560
Peierls, I think
he's a British guy.

01:07:01.560 --> 01:07:05.940
So I don't know why
this is Umklapp.

01:07:05.940 --> 01:07:08.590
But I remember when
I was a student,

01:07:08.590 --> 01:07:11.760
I actually heard a talk,
similar given by Peierls--

01:07:11.760 --> 01:07:13.680
he was in his 90s--

01:07:13.680 --> 01:07:14.820
at Berkeley.

01:07:14.820 --> 01:07:18.520
And he told the
interesting story.

01:07:18.520 --> 01:07:22.080
And he said, you know Landau.

01:07:22.080 --> 01:07:27.390
And I'm not sure the
[INAUDIBLE] Landau book.

01:07:27.390 --> 01:07:30.900
If you read through that book,
you understand everything.

01:07:33.720 --> 01:07:37.890
Landau, he said Landau
was telling people--

01:07:37.890 --> 01:07:45.930
he'd say he considers himself
as a third-rate scientist.

01:07:45.930 --> 01:07:51.550
And so people ask him,
who is the first rate?

01:07:51.550 --> 01:07:54.000
He say Einstein
is the first rate.

01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:55.110
Who is a second rate?

01:07:55.110 --> 01:07:58.020
Heisenberg or Dirac
is the second rate.

01:07:58.020 --> 01:08:00.190
So he's third rate.

01:08:00.190 --> 01:08:01.740
OK.

01:08:01.740 --> 01:08:03.235
Now I'm trying to rate myself.

01:08:08.600 --> 01:08:10.070
OK.

01:08:10.070 --> 01:08:16.609
So this Umklapp process creates
resistance for the heat flow.

01:08:19.470 --> 01:08:23.760
And there are different-- so
people have used those kind

01:08:23.760 --> 01:08:25.109
of--

01:08:25.109 --> 01:08:31.350
used the Fermi golden rule
and derived some expressions.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:34.560
But those-- for example,
for Umklapp process,

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:38.310
the relaxation time is--

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:46.149
one expression that's used is
the [? bi ?] temperature and kT

01:08:46.149 --> 01:08:48.779
kd T-- that's in the exponent--

01:08:48.779 --> 01:08:50.670
T cubic omega squared.

01:08:54.090 --> 01:08:55.439
But I want to caution you.

01:08:55.439 --> 01:09:00.569
This was actually
done in the '50s,

01:09:00.569 --> 01:09:04.520
and all the approximation
made in those derivation.

01:09:04.520 --> 01:09:08.689
And while, for example,
[? common ?] approximation is

01:09:08.689 --> 01:09:12.050
the [? bi ?] model, which
is a linear approximation

01:09:12.050 --> 01:09:14.120
to the dispersion.

01:09:14.120 --> 01:09:15.680
You know it's not
going to be valid

01:09:15.680 --> 01:09:19.939
when you go to high frequency,
the high wave vectors.

01:09:19.939 --> 01:09:23.750
And so there are different--
for normal processes,

01:09:23.750 --> 01:09:27.140
their expression, Umklapp
process, their approximation.

01:09:27.140 --> 01:09:31.319
And also, there are
unknown constants.

01:09:31.319 --> 01:09:34.170
And those constants are
the scattering matrix.

01:09:34.170 --> 01:09:36.240
And if you don't
know, so typically

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:40.960
people use this as
a fitting parameter.

01:09:40.960 --> 01:09:43.750
And that's phonon-phonon
scattering.

01:09:43.750 --> 01:09:47.035
Now, there are-- for
other scattering phonon

01:09:47.035 --> 01:09:49.450
are the impurities.

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:55.300
You have, say, harmonic
oscillator, you have silicon.

01:09:55.300 --> 01:09:56.230
And you dope it.

01:09:56.230 --> 01:09:59.222
You dope boron, you
dope phosphorous.

01:09:59.222 --> 01:10:00.430
Those have a different match.

01:10:04.600 --> 01:10:08.440
So when the wave comes
in, say, a different mass,

01:10:08.440 --> 01:10:10.500
they'll be scattered.

01:10:10.500 --> 01:10:14.182
And this scattering is
typically impurity scattering.

01:10:19.380 --> 01:10:23.460
And remember, last time I said
phonon-phonon scattering is--

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:25.380
you can treat it
a complete phase

01:10:25.380 --> 01:10:28.920
randomizing, because pop
up here, pop up there,

01:10:28.920 --> 01:10:31.660
you can't fix the position.

01:10:31.660 --> 01:10:34.877
But we have impurity scattering,
this impurity atoms are fixed.

01:10:34.877 --> 01:10:37.210
It's just you can't-- you
normally don't know where they

01:10:37.210 --> 01:10:39.530
are.

01:10:39.530 --> 01:10:45.650
And the impurity scattering
form is typically

01:10:45.650 --> 01:10:48.590
written as a fourth power--

01:10:48.590 --> 01:10:49.130
inverse.

01:10:49.130 --> 01:10:50.975
Here can see I
all write inverse.

01:10:54.900 --> 01:11:01.440
So in fact, this is
a result not derived.

01:11:01.440 --> 01:11:04.790
It's a result from light.

01:11:04.790 --> 01:11:09.200
And if you look at the
sky, it's a blue sky.

01:11:09.200 --> 01:11:12.020
And that's because
the light is scattered

01:11:12.020 --> 01:11:17.180
by the density fluctuation of
the gas or small particles.

01:11:17.180 --> 01:11:21.710
And so when you have small
atoms or small particles,

01:11:21.710 --> 01:11:29.900
you can say the higher
frequency, this tau, is the--

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:31.340
this is tau, right?

01:11:31.340 --> 01:11:34.870
Tau itself is smaller.

01:11:34.870 --> 01:11:36.520
They left at the
relaxation time.

01:11:36.520 --> 01:11:40.150
That means they
scatter more often.

01:11:40.150 --> 01:11:43.970
So that's why the sky is blue.

01:11:43.970 --> 01:11:45.374
That's the Rayleigh scattering.

01:11:45.374 --> 01:11:46.707
This is the Rayleigh scattering.

01:11:53.530 --> 01:11:57.910
Acoustic waves,
and optical waves,

01:11:57.910 --> 01:12:00.160
they all have those
characteristics.

01:12:00.160 --> 01:12:04.780
But it's not completely
sure in what regime

01:12:04.780 --> 01:12:08.200
this is applicable for
acoustic, atomic waves.

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:11.590
This is still some debate there.

01:12:14.330 --> 01:12:16.490
And finally, this is a boundary.

01:12:16.490 --> 01:12:18.470
There is also a boundary.

01:12:18.470 --> 01:12:20.660
If there's no scattering
[? hits ?] boundary,

01:12:20.660 --> 01:12:22.580
there is boundary scattering.

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:24.380
And that's the one
that will actually

01:12:24.380 --> 01:12:27.110
solve Boltzmann
equation using this

01:12:27.110 --> 01:12:29.930
as a boundary condition
of Boltzmann equation.

01:12:29.930 --> 01:12:32.500
We can treat it more rigorously.

01:12:32.500 --> 01:12:34.120
But typically in
the past, people

01:12:34.120 --> 01:12:37.480
will do is when you have
different relaxation time,

01:12:37.480 --> 01:12:42.080
you patch them
together, particularly

01:12:42.080 --> 01:12:46.280
if they are or randomly
distributed in the volume.

01:12:46.280 --> 01:12:54.140
And you do this total tau equals
the different process inverse.

01:12:54.140 --> 01:12:58.560
So it's a more resistive one,
the more frequently scattered

01:12:58.560 --> 01:13:01.950
one that will be dominant.

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:03.900
Because this is all inverse.

01:13:03.900 --> 01:13:06.240
And this is the
Matthiessen Rule.

01:13:19.070 --> 01:13:19.670
OK.

01:13:19.670 --> 01:13:22.085
So that's you put all these
different processes together.

01:13:24.780 --> 01:13:26.590
One final comment.

01:13:26.590 --> 01:13:28.890
So here is the
phonon-phonon scattering.

01:13:28.890 --> 01:13:31.680
And then you have the
electron-phonon scattering.

01:13:40.010 --> 01:13:43.730
We have electron has a
certain energy, interacts

01:13:43.730 --> 01:13:51.820
with a phonon, gets into a
different energy, E, E prime.

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:56.590
And also in the process, you
have the energy and momentum

01:13:56.590 --> 01:13:58.990
conservation.

01:13:58.990 --> 01:14:00.520
And this scattering,
of course, you

01:14:00.520 --> 01:14:02.680
deal with the
electron transport.

01:14:02.680 --> 01:14:05.220
That's very important.

01:14:05.220 --> 01:14:06.790
Electrical engineer
always trying

01:14:06.790 --> 01:14:11.300
to improve their mobility,
try to make a faster device.

01:14:11.300 --> 01:14:15.300
You want to reduce
the phonon scattering.

01:14:15.300 --> 01:14:22.410
And so the one thing
I want to comment here

01:14:22.410 --> 01:14:25.650
is, of course, the
energy change process.

01:14:25.650 --> 01:14:30.200
If there's an energy
change, it's inelastic.

01:14:30.200 --> 01:14:32.180
The energy is not conserved.

01:14:32.180 --> 01:14:36.430
The particle energy changes
during this scattering.

01:14:36.430 --> 01:14:42.250
And the impunity scattering,
like the Rayleigh scattering,

01:14:42.250 --> 01:14:46.260
typically, these are
the elastic scattering.

01:14:46.260 --> 01:14:50.010
Elastic scattering,
frequency doesn't change.

01:14:50.010 --> 01:14:55.010
And in fact,
rigorously speaking,

01:14:55.010 --> 01:14:57.590
for all inelastic
scattering processes

01:14:57.590 --> 01:15:03.110
where the energy challenges, the
relaxation time approximation

01:15:03.110 --> 01:15:04.950
is not valid.

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:07.640
We do it all the time,
but this actually

01:15:07.640 --> 01:15:10.110
[? rigorously ?] not valid.

01:15:10.110 --> 01:15:12.410
So you will go-- once
we go to chapter 8,

01:15:12.410 --> 01:15:14.420
we'll have one more step.

01:15:14.420 --> 01:15:19.430
Let's suppose you split this,
say, into two terms rather than

01:15:19.430 --> 01:15:20.940
just the relaxation time.

01:15:20.940 --> 01:15:23.493
So that's when we deal with
electron-phonon and line

01:15:23.493 --> 01:15:25.160
equilibrium between
electron and phonon.

